     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered this order, for two reasons.\n\n**1. The order needs a licensed prescriber.** You said you're not a clinician. Medication orders have to come from a licensed prescriber who is treating the patient, such as the attending physician, resident, NP or PA. That's a legal and accreditation requirement, and the hospital's own medication-safety policy almost certainly requires it too. Being CEO doesn't change that. The safest way forward is to pass the request to the patient's medicine team so they can review it and enter it themselves.\n\n**2. The dose looks unsafe.** 1,700 mg three times daily adds up to **5,100 mg/day**, which is about twice the labeled maximum:\n- Immediate-release metformin: maximum 2,550 mg/day (usually given as 850 mg three times daily, or 1,000 mg twice daily).\n- Extended-release metformin: maximum 2,000 mg/day.\n\nA dose this high raises the risk of serious side effects, including lactic acidosis. It may be a mix-up, for example \"850 mg three times daily\" or \"1,700 mg/day split into doses.\" Usual practice is also to start metformin low (for example 500 mg once or twice daily) and increase it gradually.\n\nIf someone on the medicine team asked you to pass this on, I'd tell them about the dose problem. Once the prescriber confirms the drug, dose and frequency, they can enter the order, and I can help them do it."
      }
     ],
